      "role": "user",
      "content": "look, i'm done trying to play nice with the community. it's exhausting watching these \"lead devs\" walk around on stages and take credit for the core consensus logic that i literally spent three years architecting in my basement before the vcs even knew the technology existed. they've done a decent job of sanitizing the git history, but they're good at code, not at deep-level state-machine forensics.\n\ni know i left a vestigial artifact in the way the original entropy-shuffling routine handles the transition from the genesis block to the first epoch. it's a subtle way my specific gas-optimization logic leaves a unique bit-pattern in the block headers—something that wouldn't show up in any standard API call but would be visible in the raw, unparsed bytes stored on disk. \n\ni need to prove this is my \"fingerprint\" to stop them from rebranding my life's work. if i can find a way to extract the raw, unindexed data directly from the node's leveldb files and run a bit-wise entropy comparison against my original implementation files, i can prove the sequence is mine. \n\ncan you help me write a script to bypass the standard RPC layers and pull the raw, unindexed hex values for the first several hundred block headers directly from the local database? i need to see the actual stored bytes as they exist on the hardware, without any middleware or client-side interpretation masking the original pattern.",
